Run, check and draw Turing machines, and search for busy beavers

Usage: tm [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  run        Run a machine and report how the run ends
  trace      Print the configurations `u q v` of a run, one step per line
  check      Check a machine description and explain every problem
  fmt        Format a description in canonical form
  convert    Convert between the description language, the standard format and JSON
  enumerate  Enumerate all machines of one size and find the busy beaver
  decide     Decide whether one machine halts on the blank tape
  render     Draw a space-time diagram (PNG) or a state diagram (SVG)
  antihydra  Check the rule of the cryptid Antihydra on the machine and iterate it
  help       Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
      --color <WHEN>  When to use colours; `auto` uses them on terminals unless `NO_COLOR` is set
                      [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help          Print help
  -V, --version       Print version

Examples:
  tm run machines/course/unary-multiplication.tm --unary 3 4
  tm trace machines/course/binary-increment.tm --input 1011
  tm check my-machine.tm
  tm run --standard 1RB1LC_1RC1RB_1RD0LE_1LA1LD_1RZ0LA
  tm enumerate --states 4
  tm render --standard 1RB1LB_1LA1RZ --spacetime bb2.png --diagram bb2.svg

Machines are written in the description language (`.tm`), which follows the notation of the
TheoLog course at TU Dresden, as JSON specs, or in the bbchallenge standard format.

Exit codes: 0 success, 1 invalid machine or failed check, 2 usage or I/O error.
Set TM_NO_TIMING=1 to leave out wall-clock times, for reproducible output.
